WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.010
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.550
Let's have a look at some recent results on
neural style transfer.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:12.599
You know the drill, we take a photo with some
content, and for example, a painting with

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:18.770
the desired style, and the output is an image
where this style is applied to our content.

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.680
If this is done well and with good taste,
it really looks like magic.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.920
However, for pretty much all of the previous
techniques, there are always some mysterious

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:29.680
styles that result in failure cases.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.650
And the reason for this is the fact that these
techniques are trained on a set of style images,

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.440
and if they face a style that is wildly different
from these training images, the results won't

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:40.440
be very usable.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.200
This new algorithm is also based on neural
networks, it doesn't need to be trained on

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.620
these style images, but it can perform high-quality
style transfer, and it works on arbitrary

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:53.620
styles.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:54.850
This sounds a bit like black magic.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.680
So how does this happen exactly?

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.760
First, an autoencoder is trained for image
reconstruction.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.510
An autoencoder is a neural network where the
input and output image is supposed to be the

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:06.510
same thing.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.650
So far, this doesn't make any sense, because
all the neural network does is copy and pasting

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.560
the inputs to the outputs.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.650
Not very useful.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:19.900
However, if we reduce the number of neurons
in one of the middle layers to very very few

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.960
neurons compared to the others, we get a bottleneck.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.920
This bottleneck essentially hamstrings the
neural network, and forces it to first come

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:33.010
up with a highly compressed representation
of an image, this is the encoder network,

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.480
and then reconstruct the full image from this
compressed representation.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.540
This is called the decoder network.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:45.210
So encoding is compression, decoding is decompression
or more intuitively, reconstruction.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:49.870
This compressed representation can be thought
of as the essence of the image which is a

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:55.650
very concise representation, but carefully
crafted such that a full reconstruction of

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.869
the image can take place based on it.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.030
Autoencoders are previous work, and if you
would like to hear more about them, check

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.840
the video description as we have dedicated
an earlier episode to it.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.459
And now, the value proposition of this work
comes from the fact that, we don't just use

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:16.549
the autoencoder as-is, but rip this network
in half, and use the encoder part on both

00:02:16.549 --> 00:02:19.269
the input style and content images.

00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:24.969
This way, the concept of style transfer is
much much simpler in this compressed representation.

00:02:24.969 --> 00:02:29.629
In the end, we're not stuck with this compressed
result, because if you remember, we also have

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:34.459
a decoder, which is the second part of the
neural network that performs a reconstruction

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:37.379
of an image from this compressed essence.

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:42.439
As a result, we don't have to train this neural
network on the style images, and it will work

00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:44.279
with any chosen style.

00:02:44.279 --> 00:02:45.420
Hell yeah!

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.219
With most style transfer techniques, we are
given an output image and we either take it

00:02:50.219 --> 00:02:53.730
or leave it because and we can't apply any
meaningful edits to it.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.730
A cool corollary of this design decision is
that we can also get closer to our artistic

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.280
vision by fiddling with parameters.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.600
For instance, the scale and weight of the
style transfer can be changed on the fly to

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:06.880
our liking.

00:03:06.889 --> 00:03:11.349
As always, the new technique is compared to
a bunch of other competing algorithms.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:16.409
Due to the general and lightweight nature
of this method, it seems to perform more consistently

00:03:16.409 --> 00:03:19.520
across a set of widely varying input styles.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.520
We can also create some mattes for our target
image and apply different artistic styles

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:31.280
to different parts of it.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:36.790
Local parts of a style can also be transferred.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.559
Remember, the first style transfer technique
was amazing, but very limited and took an

00:03:41.559 --> 00:03:45.419
hour on a state of the art graphics card in
a desktop computer.

00:03:45.419 --> 00:03:49.189
This one takes less than a second and works
for any style.

00:03:49.189 --> 00:03:54.200
Now, as and more new phones contain chips
for performing deep learning, we can likely

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.669
look forward to a totally amazing future where
style transfer can be done in our pockets

00:03:59.669 --> 00:04:01.540
and in real time.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.290
What a time it is to be alive!

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:07.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

